<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-swimming">
  <title>Are swimmers swimming faster?</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        "Swimming could be taught by lecturing the student swimmers in the classroom three times a week on the
        various kinds of strokes and the principles of buoyancy and so forth. Some might believe that on completing
        such a course of study, the graduates would all eagerly run down to the pool, jump in, and swim at once. But
        I think it's much more likely that they would want to stay in the classroom to teach a fresh lot of students
        all that they had learned."
      </p>
      <p>--- George Box</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p><em>Background.</em> Comparing swimmers' best times for different strokes and distances.</p>
    <p>
      <em>Questions.</em> Which strokes are swum fastest? How much faster are men than women? What effect did full
      body swimsuits have?
    </p>
    <p><em>Sources.</em> International Swimming Federation (FINA) results database.</p>
    <p><em>Structure.</em> 7,685 observations of 23 variables.</p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-swim-strokes">
    <title>Best times for different strokes</title>

    <p>
      FINA publishes competition results and datasets of swimmers' best times by event. The data used here contain
      best times for men and women across individual long-course-metre events.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-swm100f">
      <image source="images/swM100f-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Best times by male swimmers for the 50 m freestyle.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Many top performances are recent. Full-body swimsuits were permitted around 2008 and banned from 1 January
      2010, producing a visible time-period effect in several events.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-swm50a">
      <image source="images/swM50a-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Best times by male swimmers for the four 50 m events.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The event-time distributions are mostly separated, with notable outliers and exceptional swims. Breaststroke,
      butterfly, backstroke, and freestyle also differ in start and turn mechanics, affecting comparisons.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-swm50amf">
      <image source="images/swM50amf-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Best times for the four 50 m events achieved by men and women.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-sw50af">
      <image source="images/sw50af-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Boxplots of the best times by women for the four 50 m events.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Women are generally slower than men in corresponding events, but distributions can overlap in informative ways.
      Best-time distributions are typically left-skewed.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-sw200amf">
      <image source="images/sw200amf-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Best times for the four 200 m events achieved by men and women.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Over longer distances, separations between strokes change, including increased overlap between backstroke and
      butterfly. Data quality checks also revealed a likely historical event misclassification in one record.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-sw100r">
      <image source="images/sw100r-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Time differences between men and women for the four 100 m events by rank.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Rank-wise sex differences generally increase down the ranking, with special-event outliers creating visible
      exceptions.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-swim-multi">
    <title>How good are the best swimmers across multiple events?</title>

    <p>
      A small number of swimmers appear among top times across many events. Their event-by-event strength profiles
      can be compared against each event's top-200 distribution.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-khlv">
      <image source="images/khLV-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Katinka Hosszu's best times by event compared with the top 200 in each event.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-mplv">
      <image source="images/mpLV-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Michael Phelps's best times by event compared with the top 200 in each event.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Both swimmers show broad, high-level versatility, especially in medley events, though strengths vary by stroke
      and distance.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-swim-compare-events">
    <title>Comparing events graphically</title>

    <p>
      Standardizing ranked times by event enables direct shape comparisons across many events in a single parallel
      coordinate display.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-swpcp">
      <image source="images/swPCP-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Parallel coordinate plot of ranked times standardized by event across individual events.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The left edge reflects top-ranked performances and the right edge reflects rank-200 performances; variability
      among very best times is often greater than near rank 200.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-swpcpo">
      <image source="images/swPCPo-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Parallel coordinate plot aligning 100th ranks across events and sorting by relative fastest times.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Alternative alignment and ordering can expose compressed distributions and wave-like patterns linked to unusual
      best-versus-second-best gaps in specific events.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-swim-relays">
    <title>How do relay races compare with individual races?</title>

    <p>
      Relay best times can outperform individual totals over the same nominal distance because relay teams distribute
      distance across swimmers and gain multiple dive starts.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-relay400">
      <image source="images/relay400-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Best times for the 400 m freestyle events for men and women, individual and relay.</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Relay distributions are more variable because each country contributes at most one best team time per event,
      producing a different sampling structure from individual-event top lists.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-swim-countries">
    <title>How do countries compare?</title>

    <p>
      Country-level comparisons can be made by plotting female versus male representation among top-200 performers
      within each event.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-mfland">
      <image source="images/mfLand-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Numbers of female swimmers in the top 200 plotted against numbers of male swimmers in the top 200 for each
          of 17 events by country (dashed lines indicate equality).
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Some countries show systematically higher women's representation, while others show the opposite pattern across
      events.
    </p>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <em>Answers.</em> Freestyle is fastest, followed by butterfly, backstroke, then breaststroke. Backstroke
        catches up on butterfly at longer distances. The 200th fastest man is faster than the fastest woman in each
        stroke shown. After full-body suits were banned, several years passed before comparable times reappeared.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Further questions.</em> How do individual swimmer trajectories evolve over time? What is the specific
        effect of turns on performance? How do pool-length differences affect comparisons?
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-swim-takeaways">
      <title>Graphical takeaways</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Legends should be close to data and aligned with ordering in the display.</p></li>
        <li><p>Scatterplots and boxplots provide complementary grouped-data views.</p></li>
        <li><p>Sorting, ordering, and arranging improve interpretability in complex graphics.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
